<%#
App-level override of the react_on_rails_pro engine's rsc_payload template
(issue #245 — read-your-writes spike).

Plain Rails view-path precedence makes this file shadow the gem's
app/views/react_on_rails_pro/rsc_payload.text.erb (the engine has no
isolate_namespace and no view-path manipulation). The stock template calls
rsc_payload_react_component with NO async-props block, so an async-props page
refetched through RSCRoute.refetch() renders without getReactOnRailsAsyncProp
in its props and every async section throws
"TypeError: getReactOnRailsAsyncProp is not a function"
(evidence: tmp/spike-245-evidence/config1-stock, docs/rsc-read-your-writes.md).

This override staffs that door: async-props pages dispatch by component name
to the same shared emitter the initial-render view uses, so both doors stream
identical props. Everything else keeps the stock plain-props path.

Gotchas:
 * Must stay `.text.erb` — RSCPayloadRenderer renders with formats: [:text]
   and raises a bespoke MissingTemplate error for `.html.erb` overrides.
 * Every tag below uses trailing-newline trims (and this comment must never
   contain a literal ERB close sequence): the response is an NDJSON stream
   and stray template whitespace would corrupt the length-prefixed chunks.
 * @rsc_payload_component_props comes from the request query string
   (?props=...), i.e. FROM THE BROWSER. Fine for public catalog data, but a
   real app must authorize it — the 17.1 gem adds rsc_payload_authorizer for
   exactly this seam.
-%>
<% if @rsc_payload_component_name == "ProductPageRSC" -%>
<%= rsc_payload_react_component_with_async_props(
      @rsc_payload_component_name,
      props: @rsc_payload_component_props
    ) do |emit|
      product = Product.find(@rsc_payload_component_props.dig("product", "id"))
      ProductRscProps.emit_all(product, emit)
    end -%>
<% else -%>
<%= rsc_payload_react_component(@rsc_payload_component_name, props: @rsc_payload_component_props) -%>
<% end -%>
